rgWarner** (\u2713) | Power\u2011train & propulsion systems \u2013 USA | Same. |\n|  | **Harley\u2011Davidson** (\u2713) | Heavy motorcycles \u2013 USA | Same. |\n|  | **China\u202fPetroleum\u202f&\u202fChemical** (\u2713) | Refined fuels & petrochemicals \u2013 China | Uses tungsten catalysts in refining processes. |\n|  | **Romeo\u202fPower** (\u2713) | EV battery packs \u2013 USA | Same. |\n|  | **Canoo** (\u2713) | Electric vans \u2013 USA | Same. |\n|  | **General\u202fMotors** (\u2713) | Vehicles \u2013 USA | Same. |\n|  | **VinFast\u202fAuto** (\u2713) | BEVs \u2013 Singapore | Same. |\n|  | **Adient** (\u2713) | Automotive seating systems \u2013 Ireland | Same. |\n|  | **XPeng** (\u2713) | BEVs \u2013 Cayman Islands | Same. |\n|  | **Polestar\u202fAutomotive\u202fHolding\u202fUK** (\u2713) | BEVs \u2013 UK | Same. |\n|  | **Lucid** (\u2713) | Luxury EVs \u2013 USA | Same. |\n|  | **Ford\u202fMotor** (\u2713) | Vehicles \u2013 USA | Same. |\n|  | **Lordstown\u202fMotors** (\u2713) | Electric pickups \u2013 USA | Same. |\n|  | **Stanley\u202fBlack\u202f&\u202fDecker** (\u2713) | Engineered fastening systems \u2013 USA | Same. |\n|  | **Nippon\u202fSteel** (\u2713) | Steel products \u2013 Japan | Uses tungsten alloys in specialty steels. |\n|  | **NW\u202fTN** (\u2713) | BEVs \u2013 Cayman Islands | Same. |\n|  | **Li\u202fAuto** (\u2713) | Extended\u2011range & BEVs \u2013 Cayman Islands | Same. |\n|  | **Shell** (\u2713) | Refined fuels & lubricants \u2013 UK | Uses tungsten catalysts. |\n|  | **Magna\u202fInternational** (\u2713) | Body/chassis & seating \u2013 Canada | Same. |\n|  | **Apple** (\u2713) *\u2013 consumer electronics* | Uses tungsten in some high\u2011frequency components. |\n|  | *(and many other Tier\u20112 firms listed in the traversal output)* |  |  |\n\n> **Note:** The traversal tool orders companies by confidence and flags whether a filing exists on record (`\u2713`). All companies marked with a check\u2011mark could be examined further with `get_supplier_info`, but no such calls were made before the turn limit was reached.\n\n### 2. Direct documentary evidence\n\n| Source | What it says about Xiamen Tungsten |\n|--------|--------------------------------------|\n| **search_corpus (unfiltered, query \u201cXiamen Tungsten\u201d, date\u202f2010\u201109\u201122)** | Returned a set of unrelated filings (TDK, Aluminum Corp of China, Panasonic, etc.). No passage mentions *Xiamen Tungsten* itself, nor any disruption or supply\u2011chain impact. |\n| **traverse_supply_graph** | Provides only the structural relationship list shown above; it does not contain narrative text from filings. |\n\n**Result:** There is **no explicit filing or public statement** (in the corpus we can query) that confirms a disruption at Xiamen Tungsten, nor any company\u2019s own filing that directly acknowledges being affected.\n\n### 3. What could NOT be confirmed\n\n| Company | Reason for missing confirmation |\n|---------|---------------------------------|\n| All Tier\u20111 and Tier\u20112 firms (e.g., TDK, Panasonic, Tesla, Toyota, etc.) | We have not retrieved their individual SEC/annual filings (`get_supplier_info`) because the turn budget was exhausted. Consequently we cannot cite any statement that they are experiencing supply shortages, price spikes, or contract adjustments linked to Xiamen Tungsten. |\n| Companies without a filing on record (e.g., Sunwoda\u202fElectronic, Gotion\u202fHigh\u2011Tech, Samsung\u202fSDI, CALB) | `get_supplier_info` would return nothing; we also did not run targeted `search_corpus` queries for these names, so no evidence exists in the current data set. |\n| Any downstream impact on end\u2011product manufacturers (e.g., automotive OEMs, battery pack makers) | No direct mention of Xiamen Tungsten in their public disclosures was found. |\n\n### 4. Overall assessment\n\n* **Potentially affected firms:** The supply\u2011graph shows a very large network \u2013 dozens of Tier\u20111 customers and hundreds of Tier\u20112 downstream manufacturers that use tungsten in components, alloys, catalysts or high\u2011frequency parts. The most \u201chigh\u2011confidence\u201d candidates with filings are TDK, Panasonic, Minebea\u202fMitsumi, NIO, Tesla, Toyota, BorgWarner, Shell, Apple, etc.\n* **Evidence status:** The only concrete evidence retrieved is the *structural linkage* from the graph itself; no public filing or news item explicitly confirms a disruption at Xiamen Tungsten nor quantifies its impact on any of those downstream firms.\n* **Confidence level:** Because we lack documentary confirmation, any claim about the severity or breadth of the disruption would be speculative. The list above should be treated as *potential exposure* rather than verified impact.\n\n### 5. Recommendations for a complete analysis (if more turns were available)\n\n1. **Query each high\u2011confidence company\u2019s filing** (`get_supplier_info`) to look for language such as \u201csupply shortage\u201d, \u201cmaterial price increase\u201d, or \u201calternative supplier\u201d referencing tungsten.\n2. **Run targeted `search_corpus` calls** for the same companies with a filter (e.g., `company=\"Tesla\"` and query \u201ctungsten\u201d) to capture any mention that may not appear in the formal filing but is discussed in earnings call transcripts or press releases.\n3. **Cross\u2011check commodity\u2011price disclosures** (e.g., tungsten price spikes) in the filings of raw\u2011material producers (Aluminum Corp of China, etc.) for indirect evidence of upstream stress.\n\n---\n\n**Bottom line:** Based on the data we were able to retrieve, Xiamen Tungsten sits at the centre of a wide downstream network that includes many major electronics, automotive and energy firms. However, **no public filing or corpus entry currently confirms that any of these companies are experiencing a disruption**, so the impact remains *unverified* pending deeper document review.\n\nCOVERAGE GAPS (101) -- reported expli